    "berth": {
      "word": "BERTH",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "(Naut.) (a) Convenient sea room. (b) A room in which a number of the officers or ship's company mess and reside. (c) The place where a ship lies when she is at anchor, or at a wharf.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An allotted place; an appointment; situation or employment. \"He has a good berth.\" Totten.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A place in a ship to sleep in; a long box or shelf on the side of a cabin or stateroom, or of a railway car, for sleeping in. Berth deck, the deck next below the lower gun deck. Ham. Nav. Encyc. distance from it.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To give an anchorage to, or a place to lie at; to place in a berth; as, she was berthed stem to stern with the Adelaide.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To allot or furnish berths to, on shipboard; as, to berth a ship's company. Totten.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
